# ComposeIO Bus Protocol [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PROTOCOL]

> Wire-level specification for the ComposeIO semantic bus. Every transport binding — I2C, CAN, MQTT, HTTP, in-process — must implement these semantics identically. A message that works on one transport must produce identical behavior on every other transport (DP-CIO-6).

---

## 1. Bus Message Format [BP-MSG-FORMAT]

Every bus message consists of seven mandatory fields and zero or more optional fields. The semantic meaning of each field is transport-independent. Serialization varies by transport.

### 1.1 Mandatory Fields [BP-MSG-FIELDS]

| Field | JSON Key | Type | Description |
|-------|----------|------|-------------|
| Namespace | `ns` | string, max 64 chars | Dot-free, slash-free identifier. Lowercase alphanumeric + underscore. Examples: `ftl_arm`, `office_lights`, `_bus/time` |
| Message Type | `type` | enum (4-bit) | One of: `state`, `event`, `config_push`, `config_request`, `heartbeat`, `discovery`, `time_sync`, `binding`, `ack`, `log_entry`. Numeric wire codes are allocated ONCE in [BP-TYPE-CODES] |
| Value | `val` | varies by type | Semantic value. String for state/event, binary blob for config_push, CRC-32 for config_request, JSON object for discovery/binding |
| Source Module | `src` | string, max 32 chars | Unique identifier of the publishing module. Format: `{role}-{instance}` (e.g., `panel-1`, `relay-2`, `hub-main`) |
| Timestamp | `ts` | uint32 | Bus time in seconds since bus master epoch. Wraps at 2^32 (~136 years). ⛔ **NOT CARRIED on framed binary transports — see below** |
| Scope | `scope` | enum (2-bit) | One of: `local`, `global`, `remote`, `auto` |
| Sequence | `seq` | uint16 | Per-source rolling sequence number. Wraps at 65535. Used for deduplication across transport bridges, not ordering guarantees. **Not carried on CAN or UART** — same exception as `ts` |

⛔ **EXCEPTION — `ts` DOES NOT RIDE THE WIRE ON FRAMED BINARY TRANSPORTS.** [BP-CAN-ID] v2 sums to
exactly 29 bits with none spare and its data field is the value alone; [BP-UART-FRAME] is
`[0xC1][len][ns_hash][type_scope][value][crc8]`. **Neither has a timestamp field, and neither has a
`seq` field.** A message arriving over CAN or UART is **receive-time-stamped locally by the decoding
transport**. Consequently:

- A `ts` on a message that crossed a CAN or UART hop is the **RECEIVER's** clock at decode time,
  not the publisher's clock at publish time. It measures arrival, not origin
- ⛔ **`ts` MUST NOT be used to compute age, latency or freshness.** Receiver-side age measured
  from arrival, against a refresh contract declared in the device's YAML, is the ratified mechanism
  (`DEC-FRESHNESS-IS-RECEIVER-SIDE-AND-DECLARED-IN-YAML`). Subtracting a `ts` that a bridge
  stamped on its own arrival yields a number that is always near zero and always looks healthy —
  an instrument that can only report health, which is the failure mode this whole rule exists to
  prevent. ✅ **Now BUILT on the Python tier** (2026-08-15): see `[SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]`
  (`corpora/platform-specs/72-freshness-receiver.lts`) for the monotonic age counter, lazy expiry,
  the `unknown` type (wire tag `0xFD`) and the per-node heartbeat receiver. **That spec, not this
  document, is the implementable contract for freshness.**
- Deduplication by `(src, seq)` ([BP-SCOPE-DEDUP], [BP-C5]) is therefore **unavailable across a
  CAN or UART hop**, because neither field survives it

**The "modules maintain a local offset" claim is REMOVED as unimplemented.** No module computes or
holds a `_bus/time` offset — see [BP-TYPE-TIME-SYNC] §2.7, which records that `_bus/time` is
consumed by nothing. A module without an RTC does not have bus time; it has its own uptime.

### 1.2 Optional Fields [BP-MSG-OPT-FIELDS]

| Field | JSON Key | Type | Status | When Present |
|-------|----------|------|--------|--------------|
| TTL | `ttl` | uint8 | **NOT IMPLEMENTED at the core-message level** | Bridge hop limit. Default 3. Decremented by each bridge. Dropped at 0. Prevents infinite loops in multi-bridge topologies |
| Priority | `pri` | — | ⛔ **RETIRED** | Superseded by [BP-WIRE-PRIORITY] |
| Correlation ID | `cid` | uint16 | **NOT IMPLEMENTED at the core-message level** | Links a request to its response (e.g., `config_request` → `config_push`). Set by requester, echoed by responder |

⛔ **`pri` is RETIRED, not merely unimplemented.** A 2-bit, application-set message-level priority
is superseded by [BP-WIRE-PRIORITY]'s **3-bit, SERVER-COMPUTED** mechanism: `prio` is derived from
the namespace and type by one shared rule (`composeio.transport.priority_for`) and written into the
CAN arbitration id, precisely so a publisher cannot declare its own urgency. No code reads or
writes a message-level `pri` on any transport. A `pri` in a JSON message MUST be ignored, and a new
binding MUST NOT introduce one — reinstating an app-set priority field would reopen the failure
[BP-WIRE-PRIORITY] closed. (`capture.py` / `transport_replay.py` do round-trip a `pri` KEY in
capture RECORDS, but that is the capture file format persisting `msg.data["_priority"]`; it is not
a bus message field.)

**`ttl` and `cid` are specified but not implemented on the core message.** `BusMessage` has no
`ttl` or `cid` attribute and no transport reads one:

- `ttl` exists only inside the config-chunk header ([SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS], `config_chunk.py`,
  seed 8), where it is a **protocol constant** decremented per relay hop — not the per-message,
  app-settable hop limit described here
- `cid` exists only as `msg.data["_cid"]`, set by `Bus.publish_critical` / `Bus.publish_ack`
  (`python/composeio/bus.py`) and consumed by the critical-ack path in
  `python/composeio/peer/critical.py`. It is a payload convention, not a message field, and
  nothing in the `config_request` → `config_push` flow this section describes uses it

Any binding that needs either MUST implement it, not assume it.

### 1.3 Size Constraint [BP-MSG-SIZE]

256 bytes (DP-CIO-18) is a **DESIGN GUIDELINE for constrained substrates, not an enforced runtime
limit**, and nothing drops an oversized message.

⛔ **CORRECTED 2026-08-14.** This section previously said messages exceeding 256 bytes "are
malformed and MUST be dropped by the transport." **No transport does that**, and a binding written
to obey it literally would break the platform:

- `MQTTTransport.GUIDELINE_PAYLOAD_BYTES = 256` is **checked and LOGGED, never enforced**. Its
  comment is explicit that this is "a DESIGN guideline for constrained substrates (CAN frames, a
  UART line), not a runtime alarm for MQTT"
- Kilobyte payloads are **routine and intended** on the MQTT path — the Intelligence Console's
  20 bus-routed sources carry transcript narration, the deliverable ledger and the defect roll-up.
  The WARN threshold sits at `LARGE_PAYLOAD_WARN_BYTES = 256 * 1024`, reserved for a genuine runaway
- The config path deliberately moves whole artifacts far over 256 bytes (a real scene is 5,856 B)
  as sequenced chunks, which [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS] records as an explicit DP-CIO-18 exception:
  a URL presumes a peer that can dereference it, and a slaved board cannot

**The rule that IS binding** is the per-frame budget of the framed substrates — 8 data bytes on
classic CAN, 252 value bytes on UART — and it is enforced by the framing itself. Large payloads
are referenced by URL where the peer has IP, and streamed as chunked content where it does not.

### 1.4 JSON Serialization [BP-MSG-JSON]

Used by: MQTT, HTTP, in-process (Python), WebSocket.

```json
{
  "ns": "ftl_arm",
  "type": "state",
  "val": "on",
  "src": "panel-1",
  "ts": 12345,
  "scope": "auto",
  "seq": 42
}
```

Field names use short keys to stay within the 256-byte limit. All JSON messages MUST be valid UTF-8. Whitespace is optional (compact serialization preferred for MQTT).

Type enum string values — the authoritative allocation is [BP-TYPE-CODES]:

| Enum | JSON value | Numeric (binary transports) |
|------|-----------|----------------|
| State update | `"state"` | 0 |
| Event | `"event"` | 1 |
| Config push | `"config_push"` | 2 |
| Config request | `"config_req"` | 3 |
| Heartbeat | `"heartbeat"` | 4 |
| Discovery | `"discovery"` | 5 |
| Time sync | `"time_sync"` | 6 |
| Binding | `"binding"` | 7 |
| Ack | `"ack"` | 8 |
| Log entry | `"log_entry"` | 9 |

Scope enum string values:

| Enum | JSON value | Numeric (CAN) |
|------|-----------|----------------|
| Local | `"local"` | 0 |
| Global | `"global"` | 1 |
| Remote | `"remote"` | 2 |
| Auto | `"auto"` | 3 |

### 1.5 CAN Binary Encoding [BP-MSG-CAN]

CAN 2.0B extended frame (29-bit identifier, 8-byte data payload).

#### 1.5.1 CAN Identifier Layout (29 bits) [BP-CAN-ID]

**VERSION 2, ratified 2026-08-13.** v1 was `ns_hash(16) | type(4) | src_hash(9)` and is
retired. See "Why v1 was wrong" below — it is kept because the reasoning is what stops
someone reinstating it.

```
Bit 28..26  Bit 25..22  Bit 21..6   Bit 5..2  Bit 1..0
[prio]      [type]      [ns_id]     [src]     [scope]
 3 bits      4 bits      16 bits     4 bits    2 bits
```

| Field | Bits | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| `prio` | 28..26 (3 bits) | Arbitration tier, 0 most urgent, 7 bulk ([BP-WIRE-PRIORITY]). **SENDER-SIDE ONLY** — nothing decodes it, and no receiver may route on it |
| `type` | 25..22 (4 bits) | Message type enum. Codes 0–9 are allocated ([BP-TYPE-CODES]); 10–15 reserved. **The codes were NOT renumbered by urgency** — see [BP-WIRE-PRIORITY] |
| `ns_id` | 21..6 (16 bits) | CRC-16/CCITT of the namespace string ([BP-CAN-HASH]). Same width as v1, so v2 adds no collision exposure |
| `src` | 5..2 (4 bits) | An **allocated** node id, 1–15. **0 = unassigned.** NOT a hash slice. Diagnostic only — CAN does not address nodes |
| `scope` | 1..0 (2 bits) | 0=local, 1=global, 2=remote, 3=auto ([BP-MSG-FIELDS]) |

**Why `scope` is at the BOTTOM.** Directly under `type` it would become a second,
undesigned arbitration axis — and a backwards one, because `auto` (3) is the default for
anything unspecified and would sort every unspecified message to the bottom of its class.
Below `ns_id` it cannot distort arbitration between two different messages at all.

**Why v1 was wrong.** With `ns_hash` in the top 16 bits, arbitration priority was a
pseudo-random function of the namespace *name*: `throttle/left` (0x03F6) won everything by
luck, `_device/wsc6-6f4ce4/config` (0x8921) outranked `_device/hosyond-d1afe8/config`
(0xC6B6) because of a MAC suffix, and `shields` (0xCC50) — a combat-critical value — lost
arbitration to console bounding boxes. Renaming a signal silently re-ranked it. v1 also had
**no scope field**, so scope was silently lost on every CAN round trip and reconstructed as
`global` on the far side; that is disqualifying for a mothercard/daughtercard trunk, where
the internal CAN segment *is* a locality and a relay that cannot see scope leaks all local
chatter upstream.

**REQUIREMENT — one layout.** `cyd/src/bus_can_id.h`, `python/composeio/transport_can.py`
and `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py` MUST all speak this layout, and only
`transport_can.py` may declare the shifts. Two implementations that "cannot share a
physical wire until reconciled" was recorded as [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE]; it is closed and
must stay closed. Pinned by `env:native_can_id` (110 cases, all 29 bits compared) and
`python/tests/test_can_wire_format_v2.py`.

#### 1.5.1a Arbitration Priority [BP-WIRE-PRIORITY]

Three bits, eight tiers, 0 most urgent. **Tiers are named by urgency CLASS, not by
example** — a tier named by example goes stale the moment someone adds a namespace.

| Tier | Class | Assigned by | Examples |
|------|-------|-------------|----------|
| 0 | alarm — a human reacts; late is *wrong* | namespace | `alert_status`, `shields` |
| 1 | live control — closed loop, lag is visible | namespace | `throttle/*` |
| 2 | transaction control — must not be starved by what it terminates | type | `ack`, `config_request` |
| 3 | time sync — its value IS its timeliness | type | `time_sync` |
| 4 | liveness & coordination | type | `heartbeat`, `discovery`, `binding` |
| 5 | live telemetry — late is stale, not wrong (**THE DEFAULT**) | namespace | `dradis/*` |
| 6 | display state | namespace | `_app/*` |
| 7 | bulk — nothing functional may live here | type | `config_push`, `log_entry` |

**THE ASSIGNMENT RULE.** Control-plane messages take their tier from their **type**;
application data (nearly all of which is `state`/`event`) takes it from its **namespace**. A
`config_push` of `alert_status` is still bulk: the urgency belongs to the transfer, not to
the name it happens to carry.

**REQUIREMENT — a rule with a default, not a list.** An implementation MUST place an
unlisted namespace at the default tier and MUST NOT fail. The default MUST sit below every
control-plane tier, so a namespace nobody has classified can never starve an `ack`, a
`time_sync` or a `heartbeat`. Namespace rules are an **ordered** list of exact names and
`prefix/` patterns; first match wins, so the order is part of the data.

**Type codes MUST NOT be renumbered by urgency.** It was proposed and rejected: it breaks
the UART `type_scope` byte for a property point-to-point UART cannot use (there is no
arbitration on a point-to-point link), it welds message identity to urgency permanently, and
it cannot express *within-class* priority at all. `prio` makes it unnecessary.

**Keep tier 0 nearly empty.** If everything is urgent, nothing is.

#### 1.5.2 CAN Data Payload (8 bytes) [BP-CAN-DATA]

> **NOT IMPLEMENTED.** No transport in this repo emits or parses the flags/seq/TTL framing
> below; the CAN data field carries the [BP-VALUE-CODEC] value encoding directly, exactly as
> the UART frame's value field does, so an MCU speaks one dialect on both substrates. In
> particular `scope` rides in the arbitration id ([BP-CAN-ID] v2 bits 1..0), not in a flags
> byte, and the value's TYPE is carried by the [BP-VALUE-CODEC] tag rather than by a
> `value_encoding` nibble. This section is retained as a design sketch and is **not**
> normative for any current binding.

```
Byte 0       Byte 1       Bytes 2–3    Bytes 4–7
[flags]      [seq_hi]     [seq_lo|val] [value continued]
```

Byte 0 — Flags:

```
Bit 7..6: scope (2 bits)     — 0=local, 1=global, 2=remote, 3=auto
Bit 5..4: priority (2 bits)  — 0=low, 1=normal, 2=high, 3=critical
Bit 3:    ttl_present        — 1 if byte 1 high nibble is TTL
Bit 2..0: value_encoding     — encoding type for bytes 2–7
```

Value encoding types (3 bits):

| Code | Name | Payload (bytes 2–7) | Use Case |
|------|------|---------------------|----------|
| 0 | `enum_u8` | byte 2 = enum index, bytes 3–7 unused | State engine values mapped to indices. Index 0 = first value in state engine |
| 1 | `uint16` | bytes 2–3 = big-endian uint16, bytes 4–7 unused | Analog values, counters, raw sensor |
| 2 | `int16` | bytes 2–3 = big-endian int16, bytes 4–7 unused | Signed analog, temperature |
| 3 | `float32` | bytes 2–5 = IEEE 754 float, bytes 6–7 unused | Continuous values |
| 4 | `bool` | byte 2 = 0x00 (false) or 0x01 (true), bytes 3–7 unused | Binary states |
| 5 | `string_short` | bytes 2–7 = up to 6 bytes UTF-8, null-terminated | Short string values |
| 6 | `crc32` | bytes 2–5 = CRC-32 checksum, bytes 6–7 unused | Config request/response checksums |
| 7 | `raw` | bytes 2–7 = opaque 6 bytes | Extension point |

Byte 1:
- If `ttl_present` = 1: high nibble = TTL (0–15), low nibble + byte 2 = seq (12 bits, wraps at 4095 on CAN)
- If `ttl_present` = 0: full byte = seq high bits, byte 2 bits 7..4 = seq low bits (12-bit seq), byte 2 bits 3..0 = first value nibble

**CAN seq is 12-bit** (0–4095), not the full 16-bit seq in JSON. CAN bridges MUST map between 12-bit and 16-bit seq using modular arithmetic.

#### 1.5.3 Namespace Hash Function [BP-CAN-HASH]

CRC-16/CCITT (polynomial 0x1021, init 0xFFFF, no final XOR) computed over the namespace string bytes (UTF-8, no null terminator).

```python
def ns_hash(namespace: str) -> int:
    crc = 0xFFFF
    for byte in namespace.encode('utf-8'):
        crc ^= byte << 8
        for _ in range(8):
            if crc & 0x8000:
                crc = (crc << 1) ^ 0x1021
            else:
                crc = crc << 1
            crc &= 0xFFFF
    return crc
```

Source hash is CRC-9: take the lower 9 bits of CRC-16/CCITT computed on the source module string.

#### 1.5.4 CAN State-to-Enum Mapping [BP-CAN-ENUM]

> **NOT IMPLEMENTED — non-normative**, on the same footing as §1.5.2 [BP-CAN-DATA]. No transport
> in this repo maps a state value to a state-engine enum index on any substrate. **Current code
> sends the state value itself through the generic [BP-VALUE-CODEC]** — `CANTransport._encode_value`
> imports `transport_uart.encode_value` directly — so `"on"` goes on the wire as the
> length-prefixed string `02 6f 6e`, not as index `1`. The `enum_u8` code this section depends on
> belongs to the retired [BP-CAN-DATA] framing.
>
> **Why the design went the other way.** An enum index is only decodable by a peer that already
> holds the same state-engine definition, so it makes the wire format depend on config agreement —
> and a mismatched enum silently decodes to the WRONG state rather than failing. The self-describing
> codec was chosen instead, for the reason [BP-VALUE-CODEC] states as a REQUIREMENT: a value that
> must be guessed at gets guessed wrong. This section is retained as a design sketch and is **not**
> normative for any current binding.

Sketch follows. State engine values are strings on the JSON bus (`"on"`, `"off"`, `"warp_5"`). The sketch transmits them as `enum_u8` indices, the mapping defined by the state engine's value list order:

```yaml
state_engine: binary     # ["off", "on"] → 0, 1
state_engine: tri_level  # ["off", "half", "full"] → 0, 1, 2
state_engine:
  type: enum
  values: [standby, warp_1, warp_5, warp_9, maximum]  # → 0, 1, 2, 3, 4
```

Both CAN publisher and subscriber MUST share the same state engine definition (enforced at config push time). A CAN-to-MQTT bridge looks up the state engine to convert between enum index and string value.

#### 1.5.5 CAN Wire Example [BP-CAN-EXAMPLE]

Namespace `ftl_arm` state change to the string `"on"`, from node id 1, scope auto. **Computed by
running the reference encoder, not by hand.**

```
CAN ID (29 bits) — [BP-CAN-ID] v2:
  prio  = 5  (ftl_arm is unlisted -> THE DEFAULT tier, [BP-WIRE-PRIORITY])   -> bits 28..26  101
  type  = 0  (state, [BP-TYPE-CODES])                                        -> bits 25..22  0000
  ns_id = ns_hash("ftl_arm") = 0xEC85 (CRC-16/CCITT, [BP-CAN-HASH])          -> bits 21..6   1110110010000101
  src   = 1  (an ALLOCATED node id, not a hash; 0 = unassigned)              -> bits 5..2    0001
  scope = 3  (auto — it round-trips, it is not collapsed on the wire)        -> bits 1..0    11

  CAN ID = 0x143B2147

Data (up to 8 bytes) — [BP-VALUE-CODEC], the SAME encoding UART and I2C use:
  02 6f 6e     len=2, "on"          (a str is [len][UTF-8])

  Contrast, for the same semantic value expressed differently:
    True  -> 01                     (a bool is one byte)
    1     -> 01                     (an int 0..255 is one byte — see the KNOWN
                                     RESIDUAL in [BP-VALUE-CODEC]: one byte is
                                     ambiguous between int, bool and enum index)
```

Round-trip check: `decode_arbitration_id(0x143B2147)` returns `(0xEC85, MessageType.STATE,
"auto", 1)`. **`prio` is deliberately NOT returned** — it is the sender's opinion of its own
urgency and no receiver may route on it ([BP-CAN-PRIORITY]).

Note what is NOT in the frame. There is no flags byte, no `ttl_present`, no enum index and no
12-bit CAN `seq`: the retired [BP-CAN-DATA] scheme is a design sketch (§1.5.2), and `ts` and `seq`
do not ride a CAN frame at all ([BP-MSG-FIELDS]). The overhead is **zero payload bytes** — the
identifier carries the routing fields and all 8 data bytes are available to the value.

### 1.6 Message Type Wire Codes [BP-TYPE-CODES]

Every binary transport (CAN, UART, I2C, and any future framed substrate) carries the message
type as a **4-bit numeric code**, never as its JSON string. This section is the **single
authoritative allocation** of those codes. Section 1.4's table, [BP-CAN-ID]'s `type` field,
[BP-UART-FRAME]'s type nibble and [BP-I2C-ENCODING]'s type nibble all refer to THIS table; they
do not restate it and MUST NOT diverge from it.

| Code | Message type | Allocated by |
|------|--------------|--------------|
| 0 | `state` | [BP-TYPE-STATE] |
| 1 | `event` | [BP-TYPE-EVENT] |
| 2 | `config_push` | [BP-TYPE-CONFIG-PUSH] |
| 3 | `config_request` | [BP-TYPE-CONFIG-REQ] |
| 4 | `heartbeat` | [BP-TYPE-HEARTBEAT] |
| 5 | `discovery` | [BP-TYPE-DISCOVERY] |
| 6 | `time_sync` | [BP-TYPE-TIME-SYNC] |
| 7 | `binding` | [BP-TYPE-BINDING] |
| 8 | `ack` | [BP-TYPE-ACK] (01-amendment-critical-ack) |
| 9 | `log_entry` | [BP-EXT-LOG] (01-amendment-bus-extensions) |
| 10–15 | reserved | — |

**REQUIREMENT — total round-trip.** A transport binding MUST encode and decode **every** message
type in this table. `encode(msg).type == msg.type` for all allocated codes is a conformance
requirement, not an aspiration. A transport whose framing cannot carry 4 bits of type is
non-conformant and MUST NOT be shipped as a ComposeBus binding; it must widen the field, not
narrow the type set.

**REQUIREMENT — no silent fallback.** An unmappable type is a protocol fault and MUST be
reported. On **encode**, a type with no allocated code is a programming error and MUST raise.
On **decode**, an unallocated code (10–15, or a code a newer peer has been given) MUST be
reported and the frame dropped. Substituting a default type — historically `state` — is
FORBIDDEN: it silently converts an ack, a heartbeat or a log entry into a spurious state change,
which is indistinguishable on the wire from a real one. (This rule was written because
`transport_uart.py` and `transport_can.py` both did exactly that: `.get(type, 0)`, which turned
every unmapped type into `state` with no log line.)

**REQUIREMENT — one implementation.** This table is a wire contract shared by every binding, so
in any one language it MUST exist exactly once and be reused by reference. Per-transport copies
are the failure mode this section exists to prevent: on 2026-08-10 four independent copies
(`transport_uart.py`, `transport_can.py`, `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py`, and the CH32V003
firmware) each held a DIFFERENT subset of the table — 8, 2, 5 and 2 types respectively — so the
same `BusMessage` decoded as a different type depending on which substrate carried it. In Python
the one copy is `composeio.transport.MESSAGE_TYPE_WIRE_CODES` with the
`wire_code_for_type()` / `message_type_for_wire_code()` accessors. Implementations in languages
that cannot import it (firmware C) MUST cite this section by name where they declare their
constants.

---

## 2. Message Types [BP-MSG-TYPES]

### 2.1 `state` — State Engine Value Change [BP-TYPE-STATE]

The primary message type. Published when a module's state engine transitions to a new value.

- **Value**: The new state as a semantic string (JSON) or enum index (CAN)
- **Delivery**: At-least-once. MQTT QoS 1 — retain is specified but NOT IMPLEMENTED ([BP-MQTT-QOS] §6.2.2). CAN standard frame (no retry beyond CAN's built-in arbitration)
- **Idempotent**: Yes. Receiving the same state value twice has no effect
- **Retained**: Yes — ⚠ **specified but NOT IMPLEMENTED**, so a new subscriber currently receives
  NOTHING until the next publish. See the retain note in [BP-MQTT-QOS] §6.2.2
- **Rate**: On-change only. Modules MUST NOT republish the same state value. (Note the interaction:
  on-change publication plus unimplemented retain is what leaves a late subscriber empty
  indefinitely for a value that rarely changes)

```json
{"ns": "office_lights", "type": "state", "val": "on", "src": "panel-1", "ts": 12345, "scope": "auto", "seq": 42}
```

### 2.2 `event` — One-Shot Event [BP-TYPE-EVENT]

Transient occurrence. Not retained. Used for button presses, error notifications, scan completions.

- **Value**: Event name as string. Examples: `"pressed"`, `"released"`, `"long_press"`, `"error_overcurrent"`, `"scan_complete"`
- **Delivery**: At-most-once. MQTT QoS 0. CAN standard frame
- **Idempotent**: No. Each event is meaningful
- **Retained**: No. Late subscribers do not receive past events
- **Rate**: As-needed. Modules SHOULD rate-limit events to prevent bus flooding (max 20 events/sec per namespace)

```json
{"ns": "ftl_arm", "type": "event", "val": "pressed", "src": "panel-1", "ts": 12345, "scope": "auto", "seq": 43}
```

### 2.3 `config_push` — Config Binary Delivery [BP-TYPE-CONFIG-PUSH]

Delivers a compiled config binary to a module. Sent by bus storage in response to a `config_request`, or proactively after a `composeio push`.

> **SUPERSEDED IN PART by [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]** (`corpora/platform-specs/61-config-over-bus.lts`).
> The URL-reference form below assumes a peer that can dereference a URL. A node with no IP — a
> UART-slaved display, or a board behind a CH32V203 on CAN — cannot, so config also travels **as
> content over the bus itself**: a header frame, sequenced data frames, then one binary ACK, sized
> for a classic-CAN 8-byte frame. Which path applies is the device's selectable `config` strategy
> (`bus` / `http` / `cached`), NOT a property of this message type. Read that spec before
> implementing a `config_push` producer or consumer.

- **Value**: For JSON transports, a `bus://` URL referencing the config binary in bus storage. Over
  a framed binary transport, the value carries the config **content** in chunks per
  [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS], because a URL is undereferenceable there
- **Correlation ID**: `cid` is NOT IMPLEMENTED at the core-message level ([BP-MSG-OPT-FIELDS]). The
  bus-config path correlates by **namespace and content hash** instead — `_device/{id}/config`
  is both the address and the transfer identity — not by a correlation id
- **Retained**: No
- **I2C**: the "written directly to the module's config register space" path is part of the
  unbuilt I2C design sketch (§6.5)

```json
{"ns": "_bus/config/panel-1", "type": "config_push", "val": "bus://config/panel-1.bin", "src": "hub-main", "ts": 12346, "scope": "global", "seq": 100, "cid": 7}
```

### 2.4 `config_request` — Module Requests Config [BP-TYPE-CONFIG-REQ]

Sent by a module on boot (or when it detects a config version mismatch) to request its config from bus storage.

> **SUPERSEDED IN PART by [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS].** Over the bus-config path a `config_request`
> carries **nothing but its own namespace** — the request IS the address — and integrity is a
> **CRC-16/CCITT content hash** carried in the transfer header, not a CRC-32 in the request. The
> CRC-32-in-the-request handshake below is the HTTP-strategy shape.

- **Value**: CRC-32 checksum of the module's currently cached config binary. `0x00000000` if no cached config
- **Correlation ID**: `cid` is NOT IMPLEMENTED at the core-message level ([BP-MSG-OPT-FIELDS]);
  this "MUST be set" is unenforced by any binding

```json
{"ns": "_bus/config/panel-1", "type": "config_req", "val": "0xA1B2C3D4", "src": "panel-1", "ts": 12340, "scope": "global", "seq": 1, "cid": 7}
```

Bus storage compares the checksum:
- **Match**: Responds with `config_push` where `val` = `"current"` (no update needed)
- **Mismatch or unknown**: Responds with `config_push` containing the URL to the new config binary

### 2.5 `heartbeat` — Liveness Signal [BP-TYPE-HEARTBEAT]

Periodic liveness signal. Published by bus master and by individual modules.

- **Value**: Module status string. One of: `"alive"`, `"degraded"`, `"shutting_down"`
- **Retained**: Yes (MQTT) — ⚠ **specified but NOT IMPLEMENTED** ([BP-MQTT-QOS] §6.2.2), so the last heartbeat status is NOT queryable by a late subscriber
- **Rate**: Bus master: every 10 seconds. Modules: every 30 seconds. Configurable per-module

```json
{"ns": "_bus/heartbeat", "type": "heartbeat", "val": "alive", "src": "hub-main", "ts": 12345, "scope": "global", "seq": 500}
```

⛔ **THE RECEIVER WAS NOT IMPLEMENTED, AND THIS SECTION'S SILENCE ABOUT THAT IS WHY IT WAS BELIEVED BUILT.** Until 2026-08-15 `publish_heartbeat` had NO CALLER and no receiver existed anywhere in the repo — beats were neither sent nor counted — while this section, §5.2 and §9.2 all described the mechanism in the present tense with no banner, in a document that banners its other unbuilt behaviour seven-plus times. A reader's inference was therefore correct and the document was wrong. **A section that banners SOME unbuilt behaviour makes every unbannered section a positive claim of implementation.**

**Status as of 2026-08-15** — the publisher is driven by the bus clock, and the RECEIVER (`HeartbeatReceiver`, per-node liveness at 3× the declared interval) now exists on the **Python tier only**. See `[SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]` (`corpora/platform-specs/72-freshness-receiver.lts`), which is the authoritative and implementable statement; this section is retained as narrative and is NOT the contract.

⚠ **THREE DIVERGENCES between this prose and the shipped code**, listed rather than silently reconciled:
- **Rate.** This section says bus master 10 s / modules 30 s. The code declares **5 s** (`BUS_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_S`), and the interval is now DECLARED per namespace and carried in the beat payload rather than fixed here.
- **Value.** This section says a status string (`"alive"`/`"degraded"`/`"shutting_down"`). The code publishes a **dict** — `{node, uptime_s, interval_s}` — because a beat that does not name its node cannot mark a node dead.
- **Degraded mode.** Automatic entry into degraded mode on missed beats is **NOT IMPLEMENTED**. The receiver computes per-node liveness and per-namespace staleness; nothing yet drives a module into a degraded STATE off the back of it.

A module that misses 3 consecutive bus master heartbeats enters degraded mode: local bindings continue, global/remote subscriptions are marked stale. ⛔ **NOT IMPLEMENTED** — see the divergence list above.

### 2.6 `discovery` — Module Announcement [BP-TYPE-DISCOVERY]

Published by a module when it joins the bus, and periodically thereafter (every 60 seconds). Enables the bus master to maintain a device registry.

- **Value**: JSON object with module capabilities

```json
{
  "ns": "_bus/discovery",
  "type": "discovery",
  "val": {
    "module_id": "panel-1",
    "role": "button_matrix",
    "hw": "ch32v003",
    "fw_ver": "1.2.0",
    "config_crc": "0xA1B2C3D4",
    "namespaces": {
      "publish": ["ftl_arm", "red_alert", "shields"],
      "subscribe": ["_bus/time", "_bus/config/panel-1"]
    },
    "capabilities": ["button", "led", "state_engine"],
    "uptime": 86400
  },
  "src": "panel-1",
  "ts": 12345,
  "scope": "global",
  "seq": 44
}
```

### 2.7 `time_sync` — Bus Time Publication [BP-TYPE-TIME-SYNC]

⛔ **`_bus/time` IS NOT A TIME SOURCE.** It is PRODUCED and consumed by NOTHING. Do not build
anything on it.

**What is actually produced.** `Bus._publish_bus_time()` (`python/composeio/bus.py`) emits one
`time_sync` message on `_bus/time` with `value = int(time.time())` — a Unix timestamp at **one-second
granularity** — and it is called from exactly one place: `Bus.start()`, suppressible with
`announce_time=False`. It is an announcement that a bus master has come up, not a clock. There is no
60-second timer, and no other producer exists anywhere in the repo.

**What consumes it: nothing.** Every touch-point in the codebase EXCLUDES it explicitly:

| Site | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `python/composeio/peer/frame.py` | `if msg.namespace == "_bus/time": return None` — never forwarded to a peer substream |
| `python/composeio/peer/hub.py` | skipped when building the scoped state index |
| `python/composeio/peer/canbridge.py` | `if msg.namespace == "_bus/time": return` — never egresses to CAN |

⛔ **WHY IT MUST NOT BECOME ONE.** Even if something consumed it, it could not carry time:

1. **One-second granularity.** The value is `int(time.time())`. Every event inside a second is
   simultaneous, which is coarser than the ~10 ms latencies this bus is built to reason about
2. **No delay compensation.** There is no round-trip estimate, no offset exchange, no NTP-style
   correction — nothing measures or subtracts transport delay, so a receiver would adopt a time
   already stale by an unmeasured amount
3. **It cannot reach the substrates that need it.** `_bus/time` is explicitly dropped at the CAN and
   peer bridges, and `ts` is not on the CAN or UART wire at all ([BP-MSG-FIELDS]) — so the modules
   without an RTC, which are the entire justification for the mechanism, are exactly the ones it
   cannot reach

**The ratified alternative.** Freshness is **receiver-side age against a YAML-declared refresh
contract** (`DEC-FRESHNESS-IS-RECEIVER-SIDE-AND-DECLARED-IN-YAML`): each receiver measures how long
since IT last heard a value and compares that against the interval the publisher declared. This needs
no synchronized clock and no timestamp on the wire, which is why it works on the 8-byte substrate.

**Source**: only a bus master publishes to `_bus/time`; other modules MUST NOT publish `time_sync`.

```json
{"ns": "_bus/time", "type": "time_sync", "val": 1742169600, "src": "hub-main", "scope": "global"}
```

### 2.8 `binding` — Sensor-to-Actuator Binding Declaration [BP-TYPE-BINDING]

Declares a namespace binding (DP-CIO-10). Published by the config system when a binding is activated. Other modules can observe active bindings for diagnostics.

- **Value**: JSON object describing the binding

```json
{
  "ns": "_bus/binding",
  "type": "binding",
  "val": {
    "source_ns": "laser_power",
    "target_ns": "vent_fan_speed",
    "transform": {
      "type": "threshold_map",
      "map": [
        {"above": 500, "val": "high"},
        {"above": 100, "val": "low"},
        {"above": 0, "val": "off"}
      ]
    },
    "owner": "hub-main"
  },
  "src": "hub-main",
  "ts": 12345,
  "scope": "global",
  "seq": 45
}
```

Bindings can be owned by edge modules (local binding, survives host failure per DP-CIO-11) or by the bus master/service hub (global binding, requires host).

---

## 3. Namespace Resolution [BP-NS-RESOLUTION]

### 3.1 Namespace Format [BP-NS-FORMAT]

⛔ **CORRECTED 2026-08-14 — namespaces are HIERARCHICAL, not flat.** This section previously
specified `[a-z0-9_]+` with "no dots, slashes, or hierarchical separators", treating `_bus/` as a
lone exception. **That was false when written and is contradicted by this document's own sections**:
[BP-WIRE-PRIORITY] assigns tiers to `throttle/*`, `dradis/*` and `_app/*`, and the whole
device-tier identifier scheme ([SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER], `root/group/leaf`) presumes three levels. A
flat-namespace rule migrating alongside a hierarchical priority table would put two opposed claims
into the graph as equally authoritative.

- **Lowercase alphanumeric, underscore, hyphen, and `/` as the level separator**
- Maximum 64 characters
- **Hierarchical, up to three levels**: `root/group/leaf` (e.g. `bsg/dradis/contact`). A canonical
  namespace of more than three levels has NO device-tier identifier and MUST be refused at
  allocation time — never truncated or hashed ([SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER])
- **A prefix match is a level-boundary match**, never a substring one: `bus.py`'s rule is
  `namespace == prefix or namespace.startswith(prefix + "/")`, so `dradis/*` matches `dradis` and
  `dradis/contact` but never `dradis_extra`
- Reserved roots: `_bus/` (infrastructure), `_device/` (per-device control and config),
  `_app/` (application/display state)
- Config channel namespaces use `_bus/config/{module_id}` on the HTTP path and
  `_device/{device_id}/config` on the bus path ([SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS])
- Log channel namespaces use `_bus/log/{module_id}`

⚠ **The only namespace validator in the tree is `ns_announce.py`'s `_NAME_RE`**
(`^[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_./:@+-]*$`), which is broader still — it admits uppercase, dots and
colons. Nothing enforces the rule above at publish time. A binding MUST NOT assume a namespace has
been validated.

### 3.2 Auto-Resolution Algorithm [BP-NS-AUTO-RESOLVE]

When a module publishes with `scope: "auto"`, the bus runtime resolves the scope using this algorithm:

```
1. Check local subscriber table
   → If any local subscriber exists for this namespace: deliver locally
   → AND continue to step 2 (local delivery does not prevent global)

2. Check global transport availability
   → If a global transport (CAN, MQTT) is connected: publish to global transport
   → If no global transport: done

3. Remote is NEVER auto-resolved
   → Remote scope requires explicit declaration in config
   → This prevents accidental cross-network traffic
```

Resolution is performed at publish time by the bus runtime on the publishing device. The result is cached in a scope resolution table until a topology change invalidates it (module join/leave, transport connect/disconnect).

**Key behavior**: Auto-resolve delivers to ALL applicable scopes, not just the first match. A message to `office_lights` with a local subscriber AND a global transport is delivered both locally AND globally. This ensures all subscribers on all reachable scopes receive the message.

> ⚠ **KNOWN OPEN DEFECT — THE REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATION DOES NOT DO THIS. The spec above is
> CORRECT and is not to be relaxed to match the code.**
>
> `Bus._resolve_scope` (`python/composeio/bus.py`) is **first-match**: it returns `"local"` the
> moment a namespace has any local subscriber, and `"global"` only otherwise. `_send_message` then
> routes to the global transport only for scope in `("global", "auto")` — and by then `auto` has
> already been collapsed to `"local"`. **A namespace with one local subscriber therefore stops
> reaching the wire entirely**, silently, at the moment the first local subscriber appears.
>
> This is a **silent reachability bug**, not a design choice: nothing errors, nothing logs, and the
> symptom is a remote panel that simply never updates. It was ratified for repair as
> `DEC-SCOPE-AUTO-RESOLVE-IS-A-UNION-NOT-FIRST-MATCH` — **fix the code to union**, do not amend
> this section. A binding written today MUST implement the union; [BP-C3] is a conformance
> requirement and the reference implementation is currently non-conformant against it.

### 3.3 Scope Resolution Table [BP-NS-SCOPE-TABLE]

> ⚠ **NOT IMPLEMENTED.** No scope resolution table exists: `Bus._resolve_scope` recomputes from the
> namespace's own declared scope and its local-subscriber count on every publish, and there is no
> cache, no rebuild-on-discovery and no transport-connect invalidation. The design below is
> retained as the target; a builder MUST NOT assume a table is present to read.

Each bus participant maintains a scope resolution table:

```
namespace         → [local_subscribers, global_available, remote_configured]
"ftl_arm"         → [true, true, false]
"office_lights"   → [false, true, false]
"_bus/time"       → [true, true, false]
"vehicle_soc"     → [false, false, true]
```

The table is rebuilt on:
- Module boot (from cached config + discovery)
- Discovery message received (new module joined)
- Transport connect/disconnect event
- Config push (namespace list changed)

### 3.4 Explicit Scope Override [BP-NS-SCOPE-OVERRIDE]

A module's config can override auto-resolution for specific namespaces:

```yaml
namespaces:
  - ns: ftl_arm
    scope: local        # Never leaves this device, even if global transport exists
  - ns: vehicle_soc
    scope: remote       # Always forwarded to remote transport
  - ns: office_lights
    scope: global       # Always published globally, even if only local subscribers exist
```

---

## 4. Scope Routing Rules [BP-SCOPE-ROUTING]

### 4.1 Local Scope [BP-SCOPE-LOCAL]

- **Transport**: in-process function call ([BP-TRANSPORT-INPROC]) or UART ([BP-TRANSPORT-UART]).
  ⚠ The I2C mainboard ↔ daughterboard path this previously named first is a DESIGN SKETCH, not a
  transport (§6.5)
- **Delivery boundary**: Single device (one host MCU + its I2C peripherals)
- **Bridge behavior**: Local messages are NEVER forwarded to global or remote transports by bridges
- **Use case**: Intra-device communication. A button daughterboard and a relay daughterboard on the same mainboard

### 4.2 Global Scope [BP-SCOPE-GLOBAL]

- **Transport**: CAN bus or MQTT on local network
- **Delivery boundary**: All devices on the local network segment
- **Bridge behavior**: Global messages are forwarded to all global transport bridges, but NOT to remote bridges unless explicitly configured
- **Use case**: Multi-device communication. A button panel on one CAN node controlling a relay on another CAN node

### 4.3 Remote Scope [BP-SCOPE-REMOTE]

- **Transport**: MQTT over VPN tunnel (WireGuard)
- **Delivery boundary**: Explicitly configured remote peers
- **Bridge behavior**: Remote messages are forwarded to the remote MQTT bridge only. They are also delivered locally and globally (remote implies global implies local)
- **Use case**: Cross-network communication. Monitoring a vehicle's CAN bus from a home dashboard
- **Explicit only**: Remote scope is NEVER auto-resolved. It must be declared in config

### 4.4 Scope Hierarchy [BP-SCOPE-HIERARCHY]

Scopes are inclusive. A wider scope includes all narrower scopes:

```
remote ⊃ global ⊃ local
```

A message with `scope: "remote"` is delivered to local subscribers, global subscribers, AND remote subscribers. A message with `scope: "global"` is delivered to local AND global subscribers. A message with `scope: "local"` is delivered only to local subscribers.

### 4.5 Deduplication [BP-SCOPE-DEDUP]

A subscriber that is reachable via multiple paths (e.g., both local in-process and via MQTT) MUST receive the message only once. The bus runtime deduplicates using `(src, seq)` tuples. A message with the same source and sequence number seen within the deduplication window (10 seconds) is silently dropped.

---

## 5. Bus Infrastructure Namespaces [BP-BUS-NS]

All infrastructure namespaces use the reserved `_bus/` prefix. Application namespaces MUST NOT start with `_bus/`.

### 5.1 `_bus/time` [BP-BUS-TIME]

⛔ **NOT A TIME SOURCE — see [BP-TYPE-TIME-SYNC] §2.7 for the full statement and the ratified
alternative.** This section is the namespace's registration only; it does not restate the contract.

- **Published by**: bus master only
- **Message type**: `time_sync`
- **Value**: Unix timestamp, `int(time.time())` — **one-second granularity, no delay compensation**
- **Rate**: **once, at `Bus.start()`** (suppressible via `announce_time=False`). There is no
  periodic tick; the "every 60 seconds" this section previously specified was never implemented
- **QoS**: MQTT QoS 0, **NOT retained** — `MQTTTransport` never passes `retain=True` ([BP-MQTT-QOS])
- **Consumed by**: **nothing.** `peer/frame.py`, `peer/hub.py` and `peer/canbridge.py` each exclude
  it explicitly
- **Purpose**: an announcement that a bus master has come up. It does **not** deliver authoritative
  time to RTC-less edge modules — it cannot reach them (it is dropped at the CAN bridge) and `ts` is
  not on the CAN or UART wire at all
- **Failure mode**: none, because nothing depends on it. No module holds a synced offset to drift
  from; freshness is receiver-side age against a declared refresh contract

### 5.2 `_bus/heartbeat` [BP-BUS-HEARTBEAT]

- **Published by**: Bus master (to `_bus/heartbeat`) and each module (to `_bus/heartbeat/{module_id}`)
- **Message type**: `heartbeat`
- **Rate**: Bus master every 10s, modules every 30s
- **Value**: `"alive"`, `"degraded"`, or `"shutting_down"`
- **Timeout**: 3 missed intervals = presumed dead. ⚠ **The 3× is a J1939 CONVENTION, not a requirement** — CANopen and NMEA 2000 use comparable receiver-side timeouts and none derives the number from anything but experience. Implemented on the Python tier 2026-08-15 (`HeartbeatReceiver`); see `[SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]`.
- **Degradation trigger**: When a module detects bus master heartbeat loss, it enters degraded mode (DP-CIO-11): local bindings continue, global subscriptions are marked stale, remote subscriptions are dropped. ⛔ **NOT IMPLEMENTED.** No code drives a module into a degraded STATE. What DOES exist is the liveness judgement this would have to be built on — `bus.node_is_alive(node)` and a per-namespace staleness verdict — and nothing consumes either to change a module's mode.
- ⛔ **Rate and value here are STALE prose**: the code declares **5 s** and publishes a dict `{node, uptime_s, interval_s}`, not a status string. See §2.5's divergence list.

### 5.3 `_bus/discovery` [BP-BUS-DISCOVERY]

- **Published by**: Any module on join and every 60s
- **Message type**: `discovery`
- **Value**: Module capabilities object (see section 2.6)
- **Retained**: Yes (MQTT) — ⚠ **specified but NOT IMPLEMENTED** ([BP-MQTT-QOS] §6.2.2), so a new participant does NOT receive the existing registry on subscribe. Bus master aggregates into namespace registry
- **Purpose**: Enables auto-resolution (bus runtime knows which namespaces have subscribers), system diagnostics, and config validation

### 5.4 `_bus/config/{module_id}` [BP-BUS-CONFIG]

- **Subscribe**: The target module
- **Publish**: Bus storage (bus master)
- **Message types**: `config_request` (module → bus storage), `config_push` (bus storage → module)
- **Flow** (the HTTP strategy — see the cross-reference below for the bus strategy):

```
Module boots
  → Reads cached config CRC from flash
  → Publishes config_request to _bus/config/{module_id} with CRC
  → Bus storage compares CRC
    → Match: responds config_push val="current"
    → Mismatch: responds config_push val="bus://config/{module_id}.bin"
  → Module fetches binary via HTTP if URL provided
  → Module applies config, updates flash cache
```

> **CROSS-REFERENCE — [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS] supersedes this flow as one of several selectable
> strategies** (`corpora/platform-specs/61-config-over-bus.lts`). The flow above is `config: http`,
> and it presumes IP. Two things about it are not general:
>
> - **The fetch step requires an addressable peer.** Under `config: bus` the content streams over
>   the bus itself and there is no HTTP GET at all
> - **The namespace differs.** The bus-config path addresses a device at
>   `_device/{device_id}/config`, not `_bus/config/{module_id}`
>
> `config: cached` is a third strategy: flash-provisioned, no fetch. The strategy is a DEPLOYMENT
> property held in NVS — it cannot live in the scene YAML, because that YAML arrives BY the
> strategy.

### 5.5 `_bus/log/{module_id}` [BP-BUS-LOG]

- **Published by**: Any module
- **Message type**: `event`
- **Value**: Log line string (max 192 chars to stay under 256-byte message limit)
- **QoS**: MQTT QoS 0 (fire-and-forget)
- **Purpose**: Centralized log collection. Bus master or service hub can subscribe to `_bus/log/+` (MQTT wildcard) to aggregate logs
- **Not for**: Debug printf flooding. Modules SHOULD log at most 1 message/second in normal operation

---

## 6. Transport Bindings [BP-TRANSPORT]

Each transport binding implements the bus protocol over a specific physical or logical medium. All bindings MUST preserve the same message semantics. A message published on one transport and bridged to another MUST be semantically identical on both sides.

### 6.1 In-Process (Python) [BP-TRANSPORT-INPROC]

The simplest transport. Used by the Python simulator, test harnesses, and single-process applications.

- **Serialization**: None. Messages are Python dicts passed by reference
- **Delivery**: Synchronous function call to subscriber callbacks. Published message is delivered to all matching subscribers before `publish()` returns
- **Scope**: All messages are local. Global/remote scopes are no-ops unless an external transport bridge is attached
- **Threading**: Single-threaded by default. Subscriber callbacks MUST NOT block. Long-running work MUST be dispatched to a worker thread
- **Namespace matching**: Exact string match on `ns` field. No wildcards in application code (wildcards are a transport bridge concern)

```python
from composeio import Bus

bus = Bus(transport="inproc")

def on_ftl_arm(msg):
    print(f"FTL arm is now {msg['val']}")

bus.subscribe("ftl_arm", on_ftl_arm)
bus.publish({"ns": "ftl_arm", "type": "state", "val": "on", "src": "sim-1"})
# on_ftl_arm called synchronously before publish() returns
```

The `Bus` object auto-populates `ts`, `scope` (default `auto`), and `seq` if not provided by the caller.

### 6.2 MQTT [BP-TRANSPORT-MQTT]

Primary transport for WiFi/Ethernet networked devices and the remote loop.

#### 6.2.1 Topic Mapping [BP-MQTT-TOPICS]

```
composeio/{scope}/{namespace}
```

Examples:
- `composeio/global/ftl_arm`
- `composeio/global/office_lights`
- `composeio/remote/vehicle_soc`
- `composeio/global/_bus/time`
- `composeio/global/_bus/config/panel-1`
- `composeio/global/_bus/heartbeat`

The scope in the topic is the resolved scope (never `auto`). The bus runtime resolves `auto` before publishing to MQTT.

#### 6.2.2 QoS Mapping [BP-MQTT-QOS]

| Message Type | QoS | Retain | Rationale |
|-------------|-----|--------|-----------|
| `state` | 1 | Yes | State must not be lost; new subscribers get last value |
| `event` | 0 | No | Events are transient; missed events are acceptable |
| `config_push` | 1 | No | Config delivery must be reliable |
| `config_req` | 1 | No | Config requests must be reliable |
| `heartbeat` | 0 | Yes | Liveness is transient but last status should be queryable |
| `discovery` | 1 | Yes | New bus participants need the full device registry |
| `time_sync` | 0 | Yes | Latest time is always more relevant than reliability |
| `binding` | 1 | Yes | Active bindings should be queryable |

> ⚠ **THE RETAIN COLUMN IS NOT YET IMPLEMENTED.** It is the specified target and stays in this
> table — it is not to be deleted to match the code — but nothing honours it today.
> `MQTTTransport` never passes `retain=True`: both publish sites in
> `python/composeio/transport_mqtt.py` call `self._client.publish(topic, payload, qos=qos)` with no
> retain argument at all, so paho's default `retain=False` applies universally. **Every "Yes" in the
> Retain column above is currently false on the wire**, including `state`, whose own semantics
> ([BP-TYPE-STATE]) promise that "new subscribers receive the last published state." They do not.
>
> **The observable symptom**, and it is the mechanism exactly: a newly-opened widget shows nothing
> until the value next changes — for a throttle, until someone physically moves the lever. A human
> wiggling a control to populate a panel IS a manual retain.
>
> ⛔ **CONSTRAINT ON IMPLEMENTING IT — a retained message carries NO AGE.** The broker hands a late
> subscriber the last value with no indication whether it is one second or three days old, and the
> receiver's age counter starts at RECEIPT, so a stale retained value looks perfectly fresh at the
> exact moment a subscriber is least able to detect it. **Retain MUST therefore be specified INSIDE
> the freshness contract** (`DEC-FRESHNESS-IS-RECEIVER-SIDE-AND-DECLARED-IN-YAML`), not shipped as a
> standalone MQTT fix — implemented naively it re-creates precisely the lie that contract exists to
> prevent. Ratified as `DEC-IMPLEMENT-MQTT-RETAIN-WITH-AN-AGE-CONTRACT`. Preferred mechanism: MQTT
> 5's Message Expiry Interval, which the broker decrements so a retained value arrives carrying its
> own remaining staleness — **but first verify the paho/mosquitto path actually negotiates MQTT 5
> rather than 3.1.1**, rather than assuming it.

#### 6.2.3 MQTT Payload [BP-MQTT-PAYLOAD]

JSON-serialized message (section 1.4). Compact (no whitespace). UTF-8 encoded.

```
Topic: composeio/global/ftl_arm
Payload: {"ns":"ftl_arm","type":"state","val":"on","src":"panel-1","ts":12345,"scope":"global","seq":42}
```

#### 6.2.4 MQTT Bridge for Remote Loop [BP-MQTT-REMOTE]

The remote loop is an MQTT bridge over a WireGuard VPN tunnel (DP-CIO-9).

- Local MQTT broker at each site
- WireGuard tunnel connects site brokers
- MQTT bridge forwards messages on topics matching `composeio/remote/#`
- Bridge decrements TTL. Messages with TTL=0 are not forwarded
- Bridge adds its own `src` hash to a dedup filter to prevent echo loops

Configuration (on each site's MQTT broker):
```
# mosquitto bridge config
connection remote_site_a
address 192.0.2.1:1883
topic composeio/remote/# both 1
bridge_cafile /etc/wireguard/ca.crt
```

### 6.3 CAN Bus [BP-TRANSPORT-CAN]

Primary transport for wired multi-device systems.

#### 6.3.1 CAN Configuration [BP-CAN-CONFIG]

- **Standard**: CAN 2.0B (extended 29-bit identifier)
- **Bitrate**: 250 kbps default (configurable: 125, 250, 500, 1000 kbps)
- **Frame format**: Extended frame, 8-byte data payload
- **No CAN FD**: FD is not required. The 8-byte constraint enforces message discipline (DP-CIO-18)

#### 6.3.2 CAN Arbitration and Priority [BP-CAN-PRIORITY]

CAN arbitration is bit-level: lower ID wins. Under [BP-CAN-ID] v2 the **most significant field is
`prio`** (bits 28..26), so arbitration is decided by a declared design decision rather than by a
hash:

- **`prio` DOES decide arbitration.** It is a real 3-bit field at the top of the identifier, eight
  tiers, 0 most urgent. It is assigned by the rule in [BP-WIRE-PRIORITY] —
  `composeio.transport.priority_for` in Python, `bus_priority_for()` in `cyd/src/bus_wire_codes.h`
  — and every implementation MUST use that one rule
- **The namespace hash no longer sits at the top.** `ns_id` is at bits 21..6, below `type`, so it
  breaks ties *within* a tier and cannot reorder tiers. Infrastructure namespaces (`_bus/*`) still
  hash across the full 16-bit range; that is now harmless
- ⛔ **DO NOT choose namespace strings that hash low.** That workaround is the exact anti-pattern
  [BP-WIRE-PRIORITY] was ratified to eliminate: under v1 it made arbitration a pseudo-random
  function of a signal's NAME, so `shields` (0xCC50) lost to console bounding boxes and renaming a
  signal silently re-ranked it. Priority is a protocol concern with a declared rule; it is not a
  deployment trick, and a namespace MUST be named for what it means
- **`prio` is sender-side only.** Nothing decodes it and no receiver may route on it —
  `decode_arbitration_id` deliberately does not return it. Its whole effect is on the wire, during
  arbitration
- The retired [BP-CAN-DATA] `priority` nibble in byte 0 is gone. It never affected arbitration
  (it was in the payload), which is precisely why the field moved into the identifier

#### 6.3.3 CAN-to-MQTT Bridge [BP-CAN-BRIDGE]

A CAN-to-MQTT bridge is a bus participant (typically on an ESP32 or RPi with a CAN transceiver) that:

1. Receives CAN frames, decodes the binary format, converts to JSON
2. Publishes to MQTT topic `composeio/{scope}/{namespace}` — the scope comes OFF THE WIRE ([BP-CAN-ID] v2 bits 1..0) and MUST NOT be rewritten to `global`. That rewrite was a v1 artifact of having no scope field, and it leaked every `local` message upstream
3. Subscribes to MQTT topics, encodes to CAN binary format, transmits on CAN bus
4. Maintains a namespace hash → string lookup table (populated from discovery messages)
5. ⚠ **Cannot deduplicate by `(src, seq)`.** `seq` is not on the CAN wire and `src` is a 4-bit node id, not a source hash ([BP-MSG-FIELDS]) — so [BP-SCOPE-DEDUP]'s mechanism is unavailable across this hop. Loop safety is provided instead by a **provenance guard**: the bridge does not re-forward traffic it sourced from CAN

The bridge is bidirectional and transparent. Modules on CAN and modules on MQTT interoperate without knowing the other transport exists.

**One layout, imported not restated.** `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py` imports its shifts from `composeio.transport_can` rather than re-deriving them. That is a REQUIREMENT ([BP-CAN-ID]): the divergence in §6.3.4 existed because the bridge held its own copy.

#### 6.3.4 CLOSED — the CAN identifier divergence [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE]

**CLOSED 2026-08-13 by [BP-CAN-ID] v2. It is closed and must stay closed** — the same words
§1.5.1 uses, because a divergence recorded as open in one section and closed in another is worse
than either state alone.

**All three implementations now speak v2 identically**, `prio(3) | type(4) | ns_id(16) | src(4) |
scope(2)`:

| Implementation | Status |
|---|---|
| `python/composeio/transport_can.py` (`CANTransport`) | v2. The **only** declaration of the shifts; the other two derive from it |
| `cyd/src/bus_can_id.h` | v2, cites [BP-CAN-ID] by name where it declares its constants |
| `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py` | v2, importing `_TYPE_SHIFT` from `transport_can` rather than re-deriving it |

**Parity proof, not assertion.** `env:native_can_id` compares the firmware encoder against the
Python one across **all 29 bits** (under v1 the corpus covered 20 of 29 and masked the rest, which
is how the divergence survived), and `python/tests/test_can_wire_format_v2.py` pins the Python
side. A layout change that does not move both is a test failure, not a review comment.

**The historical divergence (pre-2026-08-13), retained because the reasoning is the guard.** Two
shipped implementations packed the identifier differently — the bridge as `[ns_hash 16][type 4]
[src_hash 9]` and `CANTransport` as `[type 4][scope 2][ns_hash 16][seq 7]` — and could not
interoperate on one wire. `CANTransport` carried scope in the identifier because v1 gave it
nowhere else to put it; v2 makes that a designed field at bits 1..0 rather than an improvisation.
The v1 layouts are **retired**: no implementation may emit or accept them, and the restriction
that `CANTransport` was loopback-only is lifted with them.

`CANTransport` still omits the [BP-CAN-DATA] flags/seq bytes — but that is no longer a divergence,
because [BP-CAN-DATA] is itself marked NOT IMPLEMENTED (§1.5.2) and the payload is the generic
[BP-VALUE-CODEC] encoding on every binary substrate by design.

### 6.4 HTTP (Bus Storage) [BP-TRANSPORT-HTTP]

HTTP is not a real-time message transport. It provides the REST API for bus storage (DP-CIO-23). Real-time messages flow over MQTT or CAN; HTTP is for config, assets, state queries, and system management.

See section 7 for the complete REST API.

### 6.5 I2C (Local Loop) [BP-TRANSPORT-I2C]

> ⛔ **DESIGN SKETCH — NOT BUILT. The whole of §6.5 is non-normative**, on the same footing as
> §1.5.2 [BP-CAN-DATA] and §1.5.4 [BP-CAN-ENUM].
>
> **No implementation exists anywhere.** There is no I2C bus transport in `python/composeio/`, none
> in `services/`, and none in `cyd/src/`. The register map below — `STATUS`, `MSG_LEN`, `MSG_DATA`,
> `CMD`, `CMD_LEN`, `CMD_DATA`, `MODULE_ID`, `CONFIG_CRC`, `FW_VERSION` — does not appear in a
> single source file in this repository. Neither does the 100 Hz polling loop, and no daughterboard
> firmware implements the slave side.
>
> **Do not mistake the I2C that DOES exist for this.** The codebase speaks I2C to **peripheral
> chips** — a touch controller, an I2C mux, a PN532 — through the peripheral HAL
> (`python/composeio/hal/`). That is device-driver I2C: a mainboard talking to a sensor. **§6.5
> specifies something else entirely** — a mainboard MCU exchanging whole ComposeBus MESSAGES with
> peer MCUs over a register protocol. Nothing implements that.
>
> **What this means for a builder.** Every claim elsewhere in this spec that rests on I2C being a
> live transport is a claim about a design, not a system: [BP-SCOPE-LOCAL]'s "Transport: I2C
> (mainboard ↔ daughterboard)", [BP-TYPE-CONFIG-PUSH]'s I2C config-register path, and §10.1
> Example 1 in full. The **local** scope is real; it is served by the in-process transport
> ([BP-TRANSPORT-INPROC]), not by I2C.
>
> **The substrate that actually fills this role is UART** ([BP-TRANSPORT-UART], §6.6), which is
> built, byte-parity-tested and proven end-to-end on hardware. A new local-loop link SHOULD use it.
> A builder needing this design MUST specify it properly first — in particular §6.5.3's value
> encoding cites the retired [BP-CAN-DATA] codes and would need rebasing onto [BP-VALUE-CODEC].
>
> Retained rather than deleted because the register-map shape is a reasonable starting point and
> deleting it would invite reinventing it worse.

Sketch follows. I2C connects a mainboard MCU to daughterboard MCUs within a single device.

#### 6.5.1 I2C Roles [BP-I2C-ROLES]

- **Mainboard**: I2C master. Runs the local bus runtime. Polls daughterboards for outbound messages, pushes inbound messages to daughterboards
- **Daughterboard**: I2C slave. Manages its own hardware (buttons, LEDs, relays). Communicates with mainboard via register-based protocol

#### 6.5.2 I2C Register Map [BP-I2C-REGISTERS]

Each daughterboard exposes a standardized register interface:

| Register | Address | R/W | Size | Description |
|----------|---------|-----|------|-------------|
| STATUS | 0x00 | R | 1 | Bit 0: message ready. Bit 1: config dirty. Bit 2: error |
| MSG_LEN | 0x01 | R | 1 | Length of pending outbound message (0 if none) |
| MSG_DATA | 0x02 | R | N | Outbound message bytes (read MSG_LEN bytes). Compact binary encoding |
| CMD | 0x10 | W | 1 | Command register. 0x01=deliver message, 0x02=push config, 0x03=reset |
| CMD_LEN | 0x11 | W | 1 | Length of incoming data |
| CMD_DATA | 0x12 | W | N | Incoming message or config bytes |
| MODULE_ID | 0x20 | R | 16 | Module ID string (null-padded) |
| CONFIG_CRC | 0x24 | R | 4 | CRC-32 of current cached config |
| FW_VERSION | 0x28 | R | 4 | Firmware version (major.minor.patch as 3 bytes + reserved) |

#### 6.5.3 I2C Message Encoding [BP-I2C-ENCODING]

Messages over I2C use a compact binary encoding (not JSON, not CAN format). This minimizes I2C transfer time.

```
Byte 0:    namespace length (N)
Bytes 1–N: namespace string (UTF-8, no null terminator)
Byte N+1:  type (4 bits) | scope (2 bits) | priority (2 bits)
Byte N+2:  seq high byte
Byte N+3:  seq low byte
Byte N+4:  value encoding type (same codes as CAN, section 1.5.2)
Bytes N+5+: value payload (variable, up to 200 bytes)
```

The mainboard fills in `src` and `ts` when bridging to other transports (daughterboards don't need to know their own bus-level identity or time).

#### 6.5.4 I2C Polling Protocol [BP-I2C-POLLING]

The mainboard polls each daughterboard on a fixed schedule:

```
Every 10ms (100 Hz):
  For each daughterboard address:
    1. Read STATUS register (1 byte)
    2. If bit 0 (message ready):
       a. Read MSG_LEN (1 byte)
       b. Read MSG_DATA (MSG_LEN bytes)
       c. Decode message, inject into local bus
    3. If pending inbound message for this daughterboard:
       a. Write CMD = 0x01
       b. Write CMD_LEN
       c. Write CMD_DATA
```

Polling rate is configurable. 100 Hz provides <10ms latency for button presses, well within human perception.

### 6.6 UART (Point-to-Point Edge Link) [BP-TRANSPORT-UART]

UART connects a host to a single edge MCU over a serial line — the CH32V003 development path and
the fallback when a device has no CAN transceiver. It is point-to-point, so there is no
arbitration and no addressing: framing and integrity are the whole protocol.

Documented here because it was NOT: `python/composeio/transport_uart.py` and
`firmware/ftl_jump_button/src/main.cpp` have spoken this frame format to each other since the
first CH32V003 bring-up with the layout recorded only in a Python module docstring. A wire format
that two independent implementations must agree on, held in one implementation's comments, is a
format the next implementation gets wrong.

#### 6.6.1 UART Frame Layout [BP-UART-FRAME]

```
[0xC1][len][ns_hash_hi][ns_hash_lo][type_scope][value...][crc8]
```

| Field | Bytes | Description |
|-------|-------|-------------|
| sync | 1 | `0xC1`, the ComposeIO frame marker. A receiver resynchronizes by scanning for it |
| len | 1 | Payload length: `3 + len(value)`. Bounds the frame at 255 payload bytes |
| ns_hash | 2 | CRC-16/CCITT of the namespace, big-endian — the SAME hash as [BP-CAN-HASH]. UART carries no namespace string, so a receiver resolves the name from its own registry or reports the hash |
| type_scope | 1 | Bits 7..4 message type ([BP-TYPE-CODES]), bits 3..2 scope, bits 1..0 reserved (0). Same nibble packing as [BP-I2C-ENCODING] |
| value | 0..N | Value bytes, encoded per state engine (shared encoder with the CAN binding) |
| crc8 | 1 | CRC-8/MAXIM (poly 0x31) over `len` through the last value byte. A frame failing CRC is DROPPED and logged, never partially applied |

Scope bits are the [BP-MSG-FIELDS] scope enum: `0=local, 1=global, 2=remote, 3=auto`. All four
round-trip; `auto` is carried as `auto` and resolved by the receiving runtime, not collapsed on
the wire.

The type nibble is 4 bits and therefore carries the FULL [BP-TYPE-CODES] allocation (0–9). A UART
binding MUST round-trip every one of them and MUST NOT substitute a default for an unallocated
code — see the two REQUIREMENTs in [BP-TYPE-CODES].

#### 6.6.2 Value Encoding [BP-VALUE-CODEC]

**ONE value encoding for every binary substrate** (UART, CAN, I²C), so an MCU speaks one
dialect wherever it is wired. The reference implementation is
`composeio.transport_uart.encode_value` / `decode_value`; the firmware mirror is
`bus_value_encode_*` / `bus_value_decode` in `cyd/src/bus_frame.cpp`, byte-parity-tested by
`env:native_frame` against a corpus generated from the Python encoder.

| Python type | Wire bytes |
|-------------|------------|
| `None` | *(empty)* |
| `bool` | 1 byte, `0x00` / `0x01` |
| `int` 0..255 | 1 byte |
| `int` otherwise | `[0xFE][4 bytes little-endian signed int32]` |
| `float` | `[0xFF][4 bytes little-endian IEEE-754 float32]` |
| `str` | `[len][UTF-8]`, truncated at 200 bytes |
| `bytes` | verbatim, no prefix — the frame's own length delimits it (config chunks) |

**THE VALUE IS SELF-DESCRIBING. This is a REQUIREMENT, not an optimisation.**
`DEFECT-VALUE-DECODED-FROM-BINARY-WIRE-EACAE9`: until 2026-08-13, int32 and float32 both
encoded to four bytes with no discriminator, so every decoder had to guess. The canonical
decoder guessed float (`1000` → 1.4e-42, `-5` → `nan`); `can_mqtt_bridge` guessed int
(`42.0` → 1109917696). **Negative integers were wrong under both**, so converging the two
decoders would have moved which values corrupt rather than stopping the corruption.

The tag is **in-band**, not a spare bit in `type_scope`: [BP-CAN-ID] v2 sums to exactly 29
bits with none spare, so a header bit would work on UART and not on CAN, and two mechanisms
on two substrates is precisely the fork that [BP-TYPE-CODES] and this clause both exist to
prevent. In-band also makes `decode(encode(x)) == x` a property of the codec alone.
`type_scope` bits 1..0 remain reserved.

`0xFE`/`0xFF` cannot collide with a string's length prefix because strings are truncated at
200 bytes.

**REQUIREMENT — no guessing.** An **untagged** value of four bytes MUST NOT be read as a
float32. It MUST be surfaced as opaque/raw so that a frame from a peer speaking the retired
encoding is visibly undecodable rather than invisibly wrong. Tagged and untagged peers do
not interoperate; a fleet moves in one coordinated reflash.

**KNOWN RESIDUAL, recorded not hidden.** A single `0x00` byte is still ambiguous between
`int 0`, `False`, enum index 0 and `""` — `encode_value("")` and `encode_value(0)` produce
identical bytes and both decode as `0`. Tagging it would cost a byte on the most common
value on the bus (all live throttle traffic is 0..255). Pinned by
`python/tests/test_value_codec_type_ambiguity.py::test_one_byte_width_is_ambiguous_too`.

---

## 7. Bus Storage REST API [BP-STORAGE-API]

Bus storage is an HTTP service running on the bus master (DP-CIO-23). It stores configs, assets, namespace registry, and workload definitions. All endpoints return JSON unless otherwise noted.

Base URL: `http://{bus_master}:8420/api/v1`

**Port is 8420**, from `COMPOSEIO_PORT` (`services/bus_storage/server.py`), defaulting to 8420. The
`5100` this section specified until 2026-08-14 was never the port the service listens on.

**This section is reconciled against `services/bus_storage/server.py` as of 2026-08-14.** Where the
implementation and the previously-specified shape differed, the IMPLEMENTATION is recorded as
normative and the divergence is named, because a client written against the old text fails at
runtime rather than at review.

### 7.1 Config Endpoints [BP-STORAGE-CONFIG]

#### `GET /api/v1/config/{module_id}`

Returns the compiled config binary for a module.

- **Response 200**: Config binary (`application/octet-stream`)
- **Response 404**: `{"error": "No config for module '{module_id}'"}`
- ⛔ **No `X-Config-CRC` header is set.** The header this section specified does not exist; a client
  wanting the checksum MUST call the checksum endpoint below

#### `PUT /api/v1/config/{module_id}`

Uploads a compiled config binary.

- **Body**: Config binary (`application/octet-stream`)
- **Optional header**: `X-ComposeIO-Metadata` — stored verbatim as `{module_id}.meta.json`
- **Response 201**: `{"status": "ok", "module": "{module_id}", "size": 1024}` — note the status is
  **201, not 200**; there is **no `crc` field**; and the `module` field is returned but was
  previously undocumented
- ⛔ **NO SIDE EFFECT. The documented "publishes `config_push` on PUT" DOES NOT HAPPEN.**
  `_handle_config_put` writes the file and returns; it holds no bus handle and publishes nothing.
  **A module is NOT notified of a new config by this call.** It learns on its next boot
  ([BP-CONFIG-BOOT]) or via the bus-side push path ([SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]) — which is a different
  mechanism on a different transport, not this one. Any flow that assumes an HTTP PUT reaches a
  live module is wrong

#### `DELETE /api/v1/config/{module_id}`

Removes a module's config (and its metadata sidecar) from bus storage.

- **Response 200**: `{"status": "deleted", "module": "{module_id}"}`
- **Not 404**: deleting a config that does not exist succeeds idempotently

#### `GET /api/v1/config/{module_id}/checksum`

Returns only the CRC-32 checksum (lightweight check for config currency).

- ⛔ **The path is `/checksum`, NOT `/crc`.** `/crc` returns 404
- **Response 200**: a **bare lowercase hex string** as `text/plain`, zero-padded to 8 digits —
  e.g. `a1b2c3d4`. **NOT** `{"crc": "0xA1B2C3D4"}`, not JSON, and with no `0x` prefix
- **Response 404**: `{"error": "No config for module '{module_id}'"}`

### 7.2 Namespace Endpoints [BP-STORAGE-NS]

The namespace registry is a **passive store**: bus storage persists whatever a client PUTs and
returns it verbatim. It does **not** observe the bus, so it does not derive publishers, subscribers,
current values or history. The rich response shapes this section previously specified described a
service that observes traffic; that service does not exist.

#### `GET /api/v1/namespaces`

Lists all registered namespaces.

- **Response 200**: a **bare JSON array** of registry entries — `[{...}, {...}]`. ⛔ **NOT** wrapped
  in a `{"namespaces": [...]}` object
- **Entry shape**: whatever was PUT, plus a `name` key set by the server. There is no fixed schema
  and no server-computed field

#### `GET /api/v1/namespaces/{namespace}`

Returns the stored registry entry for a single namespace, verbatim.

- **Response 200**: the entry as stored, with `name`. **No `publishers` / `subscribers` /
  `current_val` / `bindings` / `history`** are computed or returned unless a client stored them
- **Response 404**: `{"error": "Namespace '{namespace}' not found"}`

#### `PUT /api/v1/namespaces/{namespace}`  *(previously undocumented)*

Creates or replaces a namespace registry entry. **This route exists and ships**; it was missing from
this section entirely, which made the registry look read-only and left no specified way to populate
it.

- **Body**: a JSON object. The server overwrites/sets `name` to `{namespace}`
- **Response 201**: `{"status": "ok", "namespace": "{namespace}"}`
- Writes are serialised under a registry lock; last write wins

#### `DELETE /api/v1/namespaces/{namespace}`

⛔ **Does not exist.** Returns 404. There is no route to remove a namespace entry.

### 7.3 Asset Endpoints [BP-STORAGE-ASSETS]

#### `GET /api/v1/assets/{path}`

Fetches an asset (image, audio, firmware binary, etc.).

- **Path**: Hierarchical. Example: `/api/v1/assets/album_art/current.jpg`
- **Response 200**: Asset bytes with appropriate Content-Type header
- **Response 404**: Asset not found

#### `GET /api/v1/assets?prefix={prefix}`  *(previously undocumented)*

Lists assets **one level** under `prefix` (not recursive). **This route exists and ships**; it was
missing from this section entirely.

- **Query**: `prefix` — omit or leave empty for the assets root
- **Response 200**: `{"entries": [...]}` — directories first, then files, each group sorted
  case-insensitively by name. Each entry carries `name`, the slash-joined `path` from the assets
  root, and `kind` (`"dir"` / `"file"`); files also carry `size`
- **A prefix that does not exist is a valid EMPTY listing** — 200 with `entries: []`, never 404
- **Response 403**: prefix escapes the assets root (`../`)
- **Response 400**: malformed prefix (e.g. an embedded NUL)
- Dotfiles are skipped, which also excludes the `.{name}.tmp.{pid}.{tid}` temp files atomic writes
  create — so a listing never shows a torn asset

#### `PUT /api/v1/assets/{path}`

Uploads an asset. Written atomically (`os.replace`), so a concurrent GET never observes a torn file;
parent directories are created as needed.

- **Body**: Asset bytes
- **Response 201**: `{"status": "ok", "path": "album_art/current.jpg", "size": 45000}` — status is
  **201, not 200**, and there is **no `url` field**. A caller needing a `bus://` URL constructs it
  from `path`
- `Content-Type` on the request is **not** required or stored; GET guesses the type from the file
  extension

#### `DELETE /api/v1/assets/{path}`

⛔ **Does not exist.** Returns 404. There is no route to remove an asset.

### 7.4 Workload Endpoints [BP-STORAGE-WORKLOADS]

#### `GET /api/v1/workloads`

Lists all stored workload definitions.

- **Response 200**: a **bare JSON array** of the stored definitions — ⛔ **NOT** wrapped in
  `{"workloads": [...]}`
- **No runtime fields.** `status`, `hub` and `uptime` are **not** returned: bus storage stores
  definitions and has no knowledge of what is running. Liveness is the service hub's to report

```json
[
  {
    "id": "camera_frame_server",
    "runtime": "python",
    "namespaces": {
      "publishes": ["camera_front_frame_ready"],
      "subscribes": ["camera_select"]
    }
  }
]
```

#### `PUT /api/v1/workloads/{id}`

Pushes or updates a workload definition.

- **Body**: JSON workload definition
```json
{
  "id": "camera_frame_server",
  "runtime": "python",
  "entry": "server.py",
  "requirements": "requirements.txt",
  "restart": "always",
  "namespaces": {
    "publishes": ["camera_front_frame_ready", "camera_rear_frame_ready"],
    "subscribes": ["camera_select", "camera_ptz"]
  }
}
```
- **Response 201**: `{"status": "ok", "workload": "camera_frame_server"}` — status is **201, not
  200**, and the key is **`workload`, not `id`**. The server does set `id` inside the STORED
  document
- ⛔ **No side effect from this call.** Bus storage writes the JSON file and returns; it does not
  notify or start anything. A service hub that runs the workload must be polling or otherwise
  triggered — this endpoint does not reach it

#### `DELETE /api/v1/workloads/{id}`

⛔ **Does not exist.** Returns 404. There is no route to remove a workload, and therefore no
specified way to stop one through this API.

### 7.5 System Endpoints [BP-STORAGE-SYSTEM]

#### `GET /api/v1/status`

**A STORAGE-SERVICE status, not a system overview.** It reports what bus storage holds on disk. It
does **not** report module liveness, transport health or bus time — bus storage does not observe the
bus and cannot know any of those. The nested `modules` / `transports` / `workloads` health object
this section previously specified describes a service that does not exist; a dashboard built on it
would render fields the server never sends.

- **Response 200** (the actual shape):
```json
{
  "service": "composeio-bus-storage",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "uptime_seconds": 86400,
  "configs": 5,
  "namespaces": 12,
  "workloads": 2,
  "storage_bytes": 2411724,
  "disk_free_bytes": 128849018880,
  "data_dir": "/var/lib/composeio",
  "modules": ["panel-1", "relay-2"]
}
```

- `modules` is a **list of module ids that have a stored config** — derived from filenames, not from
  liveness. A module in this list may be powered off
- `namespaces` / `configs` / `workloads` are **counts**, not objects

#### `GET /health`  *(previously undocumented)*

Liveness probe. Returns `200` with the bare body `ok` as `text/plain`. Note it is **not** under
`/api/v1`.

---

## 8. Config Versioning [BP-CONFIG-VERSION]

### 8.1 Checksum Algorithm [BP-CONFIG-CRC]

CRC-32 (ISO 3309, polynomial 0x04C11DB7) computed over the entire compiled config binary. This is the same CRC-32 used by zlib, PNG, and Ethernet.

### 8.2 Config Lifecycle [BP-CONFIG-LIFECYCLE]

```
Builder edits YAML
  → composeio push validates YAML against schema
  → Compiler produces compact binary
  → CRC-32 computed over binary
  → Binary uploaded to bus storage: PUT /api/v1/config/{module_id}
  → ⛔ NO NOTIFICATION IS SENT. Bus storage publishes nothing on PUT (§7.1)
  → Module discovers the change on its next boot, or by the bus-config path
  → Module fetches binary: GET /api/v1/config/{module_id}
  → Module verifies CRC-32 matches header
  → Module writes binary + CRC to flash
  → Module reconfigures from new binary
  → Module publishes discovery with updated config_crc
```

> ⛔ **THE NOTIFICATION STEP DOES NOT EXIST.** `_handle_config_put` in
> `services/bus_storage/server.py` writes the file and returns; it holds no bus handle. A `composeio
> push` therefore does **not** reach a running module — the lifecycle above completes only across a
> reboot, unless something else polls.
>
> **CROSS-REFERENCE — [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]** supplies the live-update path this lifecycle assumed:
> the head may push an **unsolicited transfer** when bus storage changes, and boot-time pull becomes
> the degenerate case rather than a separate mechanism. Note the capability difference it records:
> `config: http` **cannot receive live updates at all** — that is a real capability boundary, not an
> implementation gap. Integrity there is a **CRC-16/CCITT whole-payload hash** carried in the
> transfer header (chosen so a 2 KB-class part needs no second polynomial), not the CRC-32
> [BP-CONFIG-CRC] specifies for the HTTP path.

### 8.3 Boot Sequence [BP-CONFIG-BOOT]

```
Module powers on
  → Reads config binary from flash
  → If flash empty (first boot):
      → Publishes config_request with crc=0x00000000
      → Waits up to 10 seconds for config_push
      → If no response: enters discovery-only mode (publishes discovery, waits)
  → If flash has config:
      → Applies cached config immediately (no network dependency — DP-CIO-11)
      → Publishes config_request with cached CRC
      → If bus storage responds with new config: applies update
      → If bus storage responds "current": done
      → If no response within 5 seconds: continues with cached config
```

> **CROSS-REFERENCE — [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS] supersedes the retry and failure behaviour here.** Two
> differences a builder MUST honour, both ratified there and both bench-verified:
>
> - **Retry is exponential with jitter and never stops** — base 1 s, ×2, ceiling 30 s, ±25% jitter
>   on every wait, as PROTOCOL CONSTANTS rather than deployment settings. The fixed "waits up to 10
>   seconds / 5 seconds and continues" above gives up. The jitter is load-bearing: it is what stops
>   a rack of boards that lost their head together from returning in LOCKSTEP and re-creating the
>   outage the moment it clears
> - ⛔ **A board with no config MUST SAY SO** — on the bus if it can, and on its own glass if it
>   cannot. "Enters discovery-only mode and waits" is silent, and a dark or stale panel is
>   indistinguishable from a working one. Silence is the one unacceptable response
>
> Also: booting from cache while re-requesting is resilient, but it can render a months-old scene
> and look perfectly healthy. **Cache MUST be paired with a visible staleness indication.**

### 8.4 Config Binary Size Constraint [BP-CONFIG-SIZE]

Compiled config binaries MUST be less than 4 KB for CH32V003 targets (2 KB flash budget after firmware) and less than 32 KB for ESP32/RP2040 targets. The `composeio push` tool validates this constraint before uploading.

---

## 9. Bus Master Election [BP-BUS-MASTER]

### 9.1 Single Master Model [BP-MASTER-SINGLE]

ComposeIO v1 uses a single, statically configured bus master. The bus master is the device running bus storage and publishing infrastructure namespaces (`_bus/time`, `_bus/heartbeat`, `_bus/discovery`).

Typically the bus master is an RPi or similar Linux host. In single-device systems, the Arduino/ESP32 host is the bus master. In the Python simulator, the `Bus` object itself is the bus master.

### 9.2 Master Failure [BP-MASTER-FAILURE]

⛔ **THIS ENTIRE SEQUENCE IS NOT IMPLEMENTED.** It is a design narrative written in the present tense, and it carried no banner while seven-plus other sections of this document did — which is precisely why the heartbeat/degraded-mode machinery was believed to be built. Step 1's DETECTION exists on the Python tier as of 2026-08-15 (`[SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]`); **steps 2 and 3 do not exist at all** — nothing enters degraded mode, nothing exits it, and no module re-publishes discovery on master recovery.

When the bus master fails:
1. Modules detect missing heartbeats after 3 missed intervals. ✅ **Detection implemented** (Python tier, `HeartbeatReceiver`) — ⚠ at the DECLARED 5 s cadence, not the 30 s this section's arithmetic assumes.
2. Modules enter degraded mode — ⛔ **NOT IMPLEMENTED**, no code path exists:
   - Local bindings continue operating (DP-CIO-11)
   - Global CAN/MQTT messages continue flowing between modules (the bus master is not a router)
   - Config requests go unanswered (modules use cached config)
   - Time sync stops (modules drift on local crystal)
   - Bus storage API is unavailable
   - Workloads on the service hub stop if the bus master is also the service hub
3. When bus master recovers — ⛔ **NOT IMPLEMENTED**, no code path exists:
   - Publishes heartbeat → modules exit degraded mode
   - Modules re-publish discovery → bus master rebuilds namespace registry
   - Modules send config_request → bus master delivers any pending updates

### 9.3 Future: Multi-Master [BP-MASTER-FUTURE]

Multi-master with automatic election is deferred to v2. The protocol is designed to support it: heartbeat messages carry a `role` field, and the election algorithm (Raft-like, based on uptime and capability) can be layered on without protocol changes.

---

## 10. Wire Protocol Summary [BP-WIRE-SUMMARY]

### 10.1 Message Flow Examples [BP-WIRE-EXAMPLES]

#### Example 1: Button Press → Relay Toggle (Local, I2C)

⛔ **SKETCH — this path is NOT BUILT.** It walks the unimplemented I2C register protocol (§6.5) and
the retired `enum_u8` encoding (§1.5.2). It is retained as an illustration of local-scope semantics,
not as a flow anything performs. The built local-loop equivalent is [BP-TRANSPORT-UART].

```
1. Button daughterboard (I2C addr 0x20) detects press
2. Button module runs state engine: off → on
3. Button module writes to STATUS register: message ready
4. Mainboard polls STATUS, reads message:
   [len=7] "ftl_arm" [type=state|scope=auto|pri=normal] [seq=0042] [enum_u8=1]
5. Mainboard local bus: auto-resolve "ftl_arm"
   → Local subscriber found (relay daughterboard at 0x21)
   → No global transport configured
6. Mainboard writes to relay daughterboard CMD registers:
   [cmd=0x01] [len=7] "ftl_arm" [type=state|scope=local|pri=normal] [seq=0042] [enum_u8=1]
7. Relay module receives, applies state: turns on relay
```

Latency: <10ms (one I2C poll cycle).

#### Example 2: Button Press → Relay Toggle (Global, CAN + MQTT)

```
1. Button module on CAN node A (allocated `src` = 1) publishes state change
2. CAN frame transmitted ([BP-CAN-ID] v2, computed by the reference encoder):
   ID: prio=5 | type=0 (state) | ns_id=0x7264 ("office_lights") | src=1 | scope=3 (auto)
       = 0x141C9907
   Data: 02 6f 6e   — the string "on" per [BP-VALUE-CODEC]. No flags byte, no seq, no ts
3. CAN-to-MQTT bridge on the bus receives CAN frame
4. Bridge decodes, publishes to MQTT:
   Topic: composeio/global/office_lights
   Payload: {"ns":"office_lights","type":"state","val":"on","src":"panel-1","ts":12345,"scope":"auto","seq":43}
   NOTE: `ts` and `seq` were NOT on the CAN wire ([BP-MSG-FIELDS]) — the bridge stamps `ts` at
   receive time and assigns `seq` itself. `scope` DOES come off the wire (v2 bits 1..0) and is
   NOT rewritten to "global"; that rewrite was a v1 artifact of having no scope field
5. Relay module on CAN node B: also received the CAN frame directly (CAN is a broadcast bus)
6. Relay module applies state: turns on relay
7. Additionally: MQTT subscriber (e.g., HA adapter, dashboard) receives the JSON message
```

#### Example 3: Config Push

```
1. Builder runs: composeio push panel-1.yaml
2. CLI tool validates YAML, compiles to binary (847 bytes), computes CRC-32 = 0xDEADBEEF
3. CLI uploads: PUT http://hub:8420/api/v1/config/panel-1  [847 bytes]
   → 201 {"status":"ok","module":"panel-1","size":847}
4. ⛔ NOTHING IS PUBLISHED. Bus storage writes the file and returns (§7.1). The
   config_push notification this example previously showed does not happen:
   Topic: composeio/global/_bus/config/panel-1        <- NOT SENT
   Payload: {...,"type":"config_push",...}            <- NOT SENT
5. panel-1 therefore learns nothing now. It picks the change up on its next boot,
   or over the bus-config path ([SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]), which is a different
   mechanism on a different transport
6. panel-1 fetches: GET http://hub:8420/api/v1/config/panel-1
7. panel-1 verifies CRC-32, writes to flash, reconfigures
8. panel-1 publishes discovery with updated config_crc
```

#### Example 4: Remote Monitoring (Vehicle SOC)

```
1. ESP32 on vehicle CAN bus reads SOC from OBD-II
2. ESP32 publishes to local MQTT:
   Topic: composeio/remote/vehicle_soc
   Payload: {"ns":"vehicle_soc","type":"state","val":"78","src":"fiat-obd","ts":56000,"scope":"remote","seq":200}
3. Local MQTT broker bridges to home MQTT broker via WireGuard tunnel
4. Home MQTT broker delivers to subscribers:
   - HA adapter maps to sensor.fiat_soc
   - Dashboard widget displays "78%"
   - Gauge module on home CAN bus receives via MQTT-to-CAN bridge
```

---

## 11. Compliance Requirements [BP-COMPLIANCE]

A compliant ComposeIO bus implementation MUST:

1. **[BP-C1]** Implement all seven mandatory message fields (section 1.1)
2. **[BP-C2]** Support all **ten** message types — codes 0–9, the full [BP-TYPE-CODES]
   allocation. (Section 2 documents eight; `ack` and `log_entry` are allocated by the amendments
   and are equally mandatory)
3. **[BP-C3]** Implement scope auto-resolution per section 3.2 — the **UNION** of applicable
   scopes. ⚠ The Python reference implementation is currently NON-CONFORMANT here (first-match);
   see the open-defect note in §3.2
4. **[BP-C4]** Enforce the 256-byte message size limit
5. **[BP-C5]** Implement deduplication via `(src, seq)` tuples
6. **[BP-C6]** Support the `_bus/` infrastructure namespace prefix
7. **[BP-C7]** Implement the config versioning handshake (section 8)
8. **[BP-C8]** Degrade gracefully on bus master failure (section 9.2)

A compliant transport binding MUST:

9. **[BP-C9]** Preserve all mandatory message fields across serialization/deserialization
10. **[BP-C10]** Implement scope routing per section 4
11. **[BP-C11]** Bridge between transports transparently (a CAN module and an MQTT module on the same namespace MUST interoperate)

---

## 12. Reserved Values [BP-RESERVED]

### 12.1 Reserved Namespace Prefixes

| Prefix | Reserved For |
|--------|-------------|
| `_bus/` | Bus infrastructure (time, heartbeat, discovery, config, log) |
| `_diag/` | Future: diagnostics and debug namespaces |
| `_test/` | Test harness namespaces (ignored in production) |

### 12.2 Reserved Source Module Names

| Name | Reserved For |
|------|-------------|
| `hub-main` | Primary bus master |
| `hub-backup` | Future: backup bus master |
| `bridge-*` | Transport bridge modules |
| `sim-*` | Simulator instances |

### 12.3 Reserved Message Type Codes

**Codes 10–15 are reserved.** Codes 0–9 are ALLOCATED — [BP-TYPE-CODES] §1.6 is the single
authoritative allocation and this section does not restate it. In particular 8 (`ack`) and 9
(`log_entry`) are allocated AND implemented; an earlier version of this section reserved 8–15 and
therefore contradicted §1.6 about two types that ship.

A reserved code is not a free code: on decode, 10–15 MUST be reported and the frame dropped, never
substituted with a default ([BP-TYPE-CODES], REQUIREMENT — no silent fallback).

JSON type strings starting with `_` are reserved.

